1
00:00:00,490 --> 00:00:05,110
"You'll never amount to anything, Aarav." Children's Day evening. Report card on the table.

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:10,770
Aarav is 15. 38 in maths. Papa's lecture starts… and Aarav switches off.

3
00:00:10,820 --> 00:00:15,780
So how do you teach a kid who won't hear a lecture?

4
00:00:15,830 --> 00:00:22,760
The Panchatantra opens with a similar problem. King Amarashakti had three sons, all three hopeless at their studies.

5
00:00:22,810 --> 00:00:27,410
The king offers a hundred villages to whoever can teach them.

6
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:36,560
An old teacher, Vishnusharma, steps up. He refuses the reward: he won't sell learning. His promise: six months, and they will know statecraft.

7
00:00:36,610 --> 00:00:44,220
He taught them not with lectures but with stories: a lion, a bull, jackals, a monkey, a crocodile. Those stories became the Panchatantra.

8
00:00:44,270 --> 00:00:51,770
Psychology calls it narrative transportation: lost in a story, we let its lesson move what we believe.

9
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:57,370
That night, big brother Kabir told Papa: "Papa, skip the lecture. Let me tell him a story."

10
00:00:57,420 --> 00:01:05,210
A later text, the Hitopadesha: an impression on a new vessel does not change; so here, through the guise of stories, wisdom is taught to the young.

11
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:09,300
Send this to the parents who only look at marks.

12
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:16,450
Your call: to set a kid right, a lecture or a story? Next Saturday, FABLE 02: the lion and the bull.
